  "body": {
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Under the present certificate grammar and checker, the answer to the proof-carrying compiler question is negative.  For every integer \\(k\\geq 1\\), there is an explicit finite acyclic target grounding and compact equality-query description whose combined pre-incidence input size is \\(O(k)\\), whose target ground graph is edgeless and hence has treewidth \\(w=0\\), and which has binary variables \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_{k+1}\\), no intervention, target event \\(B=\\{X_1=X_{k+1}\\}\\), a fair supported row at \\(X_1\\), and the unsupported binary row \\(rstar\\) at \\(X_{k+1}\\).  The row \\(rstar\\) is directly event-relevant and the replayed target polynomial is the nontrivial constant \\(F=1/2\\), while the mandated \\(Ictx\\) contains exactly \\(2^k\\) event-consistent complete target-assignment records.  Consequently, every compiler that outputs an \\(Ictx\\) accepted by \\(Check\\) has time and output length \\(\\Omega(2^k)\\).  Thus no bound polynomial in the explicit grounding size and exponential only in \\(w\\) is possible for this exhaustive assignment-level grammar.  This conclusion does not rule out a width-sensitive compiler after replacement of \\(Ictx\\) and \\(Check\\) by a compact circuit-level incidence grammar and checker.",
    "id": "thm:resolve-c220f56a3b12691186c2",
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:finite-domains",
      "ass:acyclic-groundings",
      "ass:type-sharing",
